Datatypes in C can be broadly classified into two
1) Floating point -> (contain decimal points)
float: %f (usually 4 bytes) basic datatype for storing floating point numbers
	can store upto 6-7 decimal digits
double: (usually 8 bytes) used for precision storage. can store upto 15-16 decimal digits.

float < double

2) Integers ->
int: %i (usually 4 bytes) used to store whole numbers.
	Range: -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 

short: "%hi" > (halfint i guess?)(usually 2 bytes half of int) saves memory, for small numbers
	Range: -32,768 to 32,767 
size may vary but short and int are atleast 16-bit

long: (usually 8 bytes)(4 on windows) used for big counts
	Range: -9 quintillion to +9 quintillion
hence it will be atleast 32 bits

long long: used when theres an absolute necessity that long should be 64 bit
	either in windows or linux

char --> %c or %s for string (usually 1 byte)
	Range: -128 to 127
	 in C, chars are stored using their character codes (ASCII) so technically
	they are also numbers.

general idea: short < int < long	
#Note that these are in signed format, unsigned ones will be from 0 to the double of the extremes




if an int value greater than short's maximum limit is assigned to short
then compiler will throw a warning or compile
in any case the short var's final value will be a lot different than intended

eg:-

int x -> 0001 1000 0110 1010 0000 (100,000)

short y ->   {1000 0110 1010 0000} (-31072)
the 1 at the highest bit indicates negative value in signed vars so itll be negative



dividing two ints and floating the result will give a rounded off whole number
need to typecast the other ints to float for proper result

dividing a float by int will also work since compiler will automatically
typecast the other int


Formats to live by: 

Integers

%d → int

%u → unsigned int

%ld → long

%lld → long long

%x → hex

Characters / Strings

%c → char

%s → string

Floating point

%f → normal float/double printing

%e → scientific notation

%g → “smart” (auto chooses between %f and %e)

Sizes / pointers

%zu → size_t (used with sizeof)

%p → pointer address
